Subject: A compiled frame's register file is published to the collector

What this does
  - A light frame is sized and scanned by `regvm_frame_regs(cb)`, which is the
    program's own `regvm_reg_count` widened to the count the unit compiled the
    body for. The generator publishes that count per body in
    `qjs_aot_table[].reg_count` (new field in `quickjs-aot-rt.h`), and a body
    that cannot publish one keeps the program's own count, so nothing widens.
  - `regvm_light_scan` is that count, kept separate from `regvm_reg_count`
    because the latter also POSITIONS `var_refs` and the handshake slots in the
    interpreter's flat frame (`out->var_refs = out->regs + b->regvm_reg_count +
    REGVM_HS`); raising it would move them.
  - `regvm_set_frame_shape` recomputes the light-frame size, `regvm_end_off` and
    `regvm_frame_need` at bind, so a frame is allocated for the widened count.
  - `regvm_light_fill` initialises the whole widened file.
  - Also: `js_aot_enter_count` is incremented on both fast AOT entries (the
    `aot_nfn` path and the light entry), so `enter` in the unit stats counts code
    that is really running instead of reporting 0.

Why we need it
  A virtual object's argument registers and a hoisted global read are allocated
  ABOVE the program's own register count, and a compiled body writes counted
  values into them. The collector scanned only `regvm_reg_count` and the
  pre-safepoint sync stopped at the program's registers, so a collection between
  a construct and the last read of a member could recycle an object those
  registers still held. Publishing the count is what lets the sync and the scan
  cover them, and it is what makes the virtual-object safepoint relaxation
  sound: without it, vec3 is a heap-use-after-free under GC-at-every-malloc.

Functions touched
  `regvm_frame_regs` (new), `regvm_set_frame_shape`, `regvm_light_fill`,
  `regvm_light_walk`, `regvm_end_off`, `regvm_frame_need`, `js_aot_bind`,
  `qjs_aot_note_frame_regs` / `qjs_aot_frame_regs`, the two fast AOT entries.

No.

